    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Evaluate \( \displaystyle \int ({@func_var_u@}){@func_var_dv@} dx\)</span><br></p>
<p>Step 1: The first step here is to pick \(u\) and \(dv\) such that \( \displaystyle \int u\cdot dv = u \cdot v - \int v\cdot du\).</p><p>
<br><p>Step 2: For this problem well use the following choices for \(u\) and \(dv\)</p>
<p></p>
<p>\(u =\)[[input:ans1a]] and \(dv=\)[[input:ans1b]]  [[validation:ans1a]][[feedback:prt1a]][[validation:ans1b]][[feedback:prt1b]], <br> Next, we need to compute \(du\) (by differentiating \(u\)) and \(v\) (by integrating \(\frac{du}{dx}\)).</p>
<p>\(\frac{du}{dx}=\)[[input:ans1c]] and \(v=\)[[input:ans1d]] [[validation:ans1c]][[feedback:prt1c]] [[validation:ans1d]][[feedback:prt1d]]</p>
<p>Step 3: Plugging the above back into the Integration by Parts formula \( \displaystyle \int u\cdot dv = u \cdot v - \int v\cdot du\):</p>

<p>\( \displaystyle \int {@u_dv@} \;dx =\) [[input:ans2a]]  \(- \displaystyle \int \) [[input:ans2b]] \( dx\) [[validation:ans2a]][[validation:ans2b]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p> 
</p>
<p>Step 4: To evaluate the integral \(\displaystyle \int {@ta2b@} \;dx\), we will need to apply integration by parts using the formula   \( \displaystyle \int s\cdot dt = s \cdot t - \int t\cdot ds\).</p> </p>
<p>\(s=\)[[input:ans3a]] and \(\frac{dt}{dx}=\)[[input:ans3b]]</p>
<br>
<p>\(ds=\)[[input:ans3c]] and \(t=\)[[input:ans3d]][[validation:ans3a]][[validation:ans3b]][[validation:ans3c]][[validation:ans3d]][[feedback:prt3]]</p>
<p>Now <br> \( \displaystyle \int  {@ta2b@} \;dx =\) [[input:ans4a]]  \(- \displaystyle \int \) [[input:ans4b]] \( dx\) [[validation:ans4a]][[validation:ans4b]] [[feedback:prt4]]</p>

<p> Now comparing step 4 to step 3, we conclude that \(\displaystyle \int {@func_var_u*func_var_dv@} \;dx=\)[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]][[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Worked out solution<br></p>
<p>Step 1: The first step here is to pick \(u\) and \(dv\) such that \( \displaystyle \int udv = u \cdot v - \int vdu\).</p><p><br><p>Step 2: For this problem well use the following choices for \(u\) and \(dv\)</p>
<p></p>
<p>\(u = {@func_var_dv@}\), \(dv ={@func_var_u@} dx\), <br> Next, we need to compute \(du\) (by differentiating \(u\)) and \(v\) (by integrating \(dv\)).</p>
<p>\(\frac{du}{dx}= {@diff_u@}\), \(v= {@int_v@}\)</p>

<p>Step 3: Plugging the above back into the Integration by Parts formula \( \displaystyle \int udv = u \cdot v - \int vdu\):</p>

<p>\( \displaystyle \int ({@func_var_u@}){@func_var_dv@} dx = ({@int_v@})({@func_var_dv@}) - \displaystyle \int {@int_v@})({@diff_u@} dx\)</p> <br>

<p>Step 4: To evaluate the integral \(\displaystyle \int {@ta2b@} \;dx\), we will need to apply integration by parts using the formula   \( \displaystyle \int s\cdot dt = s \cdot t - \int t\cdot ds\).</p> </p>
<p>\(s={@ta4a@}\) and \(\frac{dt}{dx}={@ta3b@}dx\)</p>
<br>
<p>\(ds={@ta3c@}\) and \(t={@ta3d@} \)</p>

<br>
<p>Now</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \int ({@int_v@})({@diff_u@}) dx =({@diff_u@})({@int(int_v,x)@})-\displaystyle \int({@int(int_v,x)@}) ({@diff(diff_u)/del(x)@})dx\) </p>
<br>
<p>\(\displaystyle \int ({@func_var_u@}){@func_var_dv@} dx =({@int_v@})({@func_var_dv@})+({@diff_u@})({@int(int_v,x)@})-\displaystyle \int {@int(int_v,x)@} \times{@diff(diff_u)@}\)</p>
<p>Step 5: Rearranging and simplifying the above function we have the final answer,</p>
<br>
<p> \( \displaystyle \int ({@func_var_u@}){@func_var_dv@} dx={@ta@}\)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
